\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (H, 5) is excellent. It places a piece on the bottom edge where I already own both corners (H1 and H8). It flips the opponent's piece at H4. Because I own the corners, the pieces on this edge become stable (they cannot be flanked and flipped back), securing permanent territory.\n- Move (D, 1) is a decent edge move that flips three pieces (D2-D4), but since I do not own the adjacent corner A1, the piece at D1 is not stable and could be a liability later.\n- Moves like (A, 7) or (B, 7) are risky as they place pieces adjacent to the empty corner A8, potentially giving the opponent access to that corner.\n\nReason: The move (H, 5) leverages my existing control of the H1 and H8 corners to completely secure the H-row. It captures the opponent's only piece on that row and converts the line into stable discs that can never be flipped back, solidifying my lead without offering any counter-play opportunities to the opponent.\n\nAnswer: (H, 5)"}
