.\n\n6.  I need to re-evaluate the moves to be sure. G6 is the best.\n\nSo, comparing: A7 is risky. G6 is great. B4 is okay. E7 is ok but G6 does more. G3, G5 less important. G6 is the strongest move by far, so that is my move (G, 6).\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the late-middle stage with me playing as \'*\'. I hold a dominant position, controlling two corners (H1, H8), the entire D row, and most of the right edge (Column 8). The opponent (\'@\') is concentrated in rows E and F. My objective is to consolidate my territory, limit opponent mobility, and avoid giving up corners.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (A, 7) is a move to a "C-square" (diagonally adjacent to the empty corner A8). This is strategically dangerous as it often provides the opponent a mechanism to capture the corner.\n- Move (B, 4) is a decent internal move that flips one piece but does not significantly improve my structural control.\n- Move (G, 6) is a strong move that places a piece adjacent to my secure block in the bottom-right (G7, G8, F7, F8, H8). It captures three opponent pieces (F5, F6, E6), effectively breaking the opponent\'s wall on row F and expanding my stable territory.\n\nReason: I chose (G, 6) because it is the most stable and constructive move. It connects to my existing strongholds in the bottom-right and the H8 corner, significantly reducing the opponent\'s presence in the lower-center of the board. It avoids the high risk associated with move (A, 7) near the uncaptured corner.\n\nAnswer: (G, 6)'}
