ontrol key areas without extending myself too early. I finally make sure the given output parameters are accurate. This demonstrates a deep understanding of Reversi strategy and an ability to analyze complex board states efficiently.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the opening phase. The opponent has established a horizontal wall of pieces from D3 to D6. My pieces are positioned at C4 and E4-E6. The current goal is to select a move that strengthens my position in the center while limiting the opponent\'s available responses (mobility).\n\nEvaluation:\nThe valid moves are (C, 2), (C, 3), (C, 5), (C, 6), (C, 7), and (E, 2).\n1.  **Outer moves (C, 2), (C, 7), (E, 2):** These moves place pieces on the outer rim of the central group. This expands my "frontier" (the set of empty squares adjacent to my pieces), giving the opponent more opportunities to place pieces and flip mine. These are generally weaker in the opening.\n2.  **Central moves (C, 3), (C, 5), (C, 6):** These stay within the 4x4 center box, which is preferable.\n    *   **(C, 6):** Flips two discs (D5 and D6). Flipping multiple discs early often opens up too many new lines for the opponent.\n    *   **(C, 5):** Flips D5. This leaves the opponent\'s pieces at D3 and D4 connected. Analysis shows this leaves the opponent with many valid responses (e.g., B4, D2, E3, F4).\n    *   **(C, 3):** Flips D4. This isolates the opponent\'s piece at D3 and leaves D5/D6 on the other side. Critically, this move drastically reduces the opponent\'s legal moves, restricting them almost entirely to the F-column (e.g., F4, F5, F6).\n\nReason: The move at (C, 3) is the best strategic choice because it maximizes "relative mobility." By playing C3, I flip only one disc (D4) and keep my position compact. More importantly, this move severely restricts the opponent\'s options, forcing them into a narrow set of moves on the right side of the board, allowing me to dictate the flow of the game.\n\nAnswer: (C, 3)'}
