rner at H1 and creates a solid, hard-to-attack edge. It doesn\'t give away a corner and restricts the opponent. No immediate risk.\n\nAll other options seem either too risky (giving the opponent a corner or a way in) or not solid enough. My H1 is solid.\n\nThe strategy is to stabilize the board, create lines of defense, and then gradually expand from a strong base.\n\n**(H, 3) is the move.**\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the mid-to-late stage. I hold the H1 and A8 corners. The H1 corner provides a strong anchor for the bottom edge (Row H). The opponent has presence in the center and on the A-row and F-row. My control of column 8 is interrupted at E8 and G8/H8.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Moves adjacent to the A1 corner (A2, B1, B2) are dangerous as they might concede the corner to the opponent.\n- Moves on the H-row, specifically (H, 3), leverage the secure H1 corner.\n- (H, 3) flips H2, creating a connected line of H1(*)-H2(*)-H3(*). Since H1 is my corner, this block is stable and cannot be flanked from the left.\n- (H, 3) does not offer the opponent any obvious counter-attack or corner access (H4 is not immediately playable for the opponent as they lack a bracketing piece).\n- Other moves like (A, 5) or (D, 1) create unstable edges that the opponent can easily flip back.\n- (F, 8) is a valid edge move but places a piece adjacent to the empty H8 corner, which adds unnecessary risk.\n\nReason: The move (H, 3) is a "quiet move" that extends territory along an edge starting from a captured corner. It is extremely stable, maximizes safety, and limits the opponent\'s options in that sector. It avoids the risks associated with C-squares and X-squares near empty corners.\n\nAnswer: (H, 3)'}
