rner.\n*   **(H, 8):** **The corner!**  This is an immediately attractive option. Corners are the most stable pieces and give you a huge advantage. Taking the bottom-right corner immediately secures that entire edge. No downsides here! Taking H8 links my two current corners and effectively gives me a dominant position.\n\nI triple-check that taking H8 is a valid move (it is, looking at the diagonal) and that the game logic confirms it, not allowing the opponent to flip it. It's the best move by far. It gives me a strong strategic advantage, secures a corner and prevents it to the opponent, and sets me up for a potential win.  Absolutely the best choice.\n\nI'm confident in my answer!\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: The game is in the middle-to-late stage. I currently hold two corners (A8 and H1) and have a solid wall of pieces along the bottom row (row H) and the right side of the top row (row A). The opponent has a concentration of pieces in the center and right-middle.\n\nEvaluation: The list of valid moves includes (H, 8), which is a corner position. In Reversi, corner moves are strategically superior because a piece placed in a corner cannot be flipped for the rest of the game.\n- Move (H, 8) captures the corner using the diagonal line from E5 (*) through F6 (@) and G7 (@). This not only secures a permanent piece but also connects my control of the right edge (via A8) and the bottom edge (via H1).\n- Moves like (G, 8) or (H, 7) are dangerous as they place pieces adjacent to the corner (C-squares or X-squares), potentially giving the opponent access to H8 on a later turn.\n- Moves like (A, 2) and (B, 2) are also risky C-square moves near the A1 corner.\n- Move (H, 6) is a safe edge extension, but it is inferior to taking the corner immediately.\n\nReason: The move (H, 8) is the best choice because it claims a stable corner. Securing H8 consolidates my control over the entire bottom edge (connecting to H1) and the right edge (connecting to A8), creating a powerful s